|~ `::qlang`, the topic of the language, and what is declared under it:
   its page, the first screen a session reads, the refusal of an answer
   past the budget, its chapters, and their laws.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence], [QSSLF#page], [QSERR#site]. ~|

:qlang
  |~~ qlang is a pipeline query language over values. A query starts
      from a subject, the value it is given, and passes it through steps:
      each step takes the value the step before answered and answers a
      new one, and the last answer is the answer of the query. No value
      ever changes. Everything the language handles is a value with
      a literal that prints and reads back as itself, data, code, prose
      and failures alike, so the language describes itself in its own
      values: every name has a page, the pages grow from ::qlang, and a
      query reads them as it reads any data.

      1. The pipe. `a | b` runs b on the value a answered, its subject:
      `[3 1 2] | sort | first` answers `1`. Each step is a command, a
      verb and what it takes, and a value stands where it is the
      subject, at the head or after a declaration, or as what a verb
      takes:
      `get /users | filter /active | sort /age | map /name` reads the
      users, keeps the active ones, ranks them by age and answers their
      names. A value written as a later step is refused.

      2. Values. Every value has a kind, named by its tag, which `tag`
      answers: `[1 2] | tag` answers `::vec`. A value is written as its
      literal, which opens with the sign of its kind and closes with its
      pair, and everything between is literal: a literal reads no name and
      no subject, so it is the value it writes. Numbers, ::number, are
      finite doubles: `42`, `-1.5`. Strings, ::string: `"text"`.
      Keywords, ::keyword, are names that stand for themselves: `:name`.
      Booleans and null: `true`, `false`, `null`. Vectors, ::vec:
      `[1 2 3]`. Maps, ::map, have keyword keys: `{:id 7 :qty 2}`. Sets,
      ::set, hold no duplicates and keep one order: `#[3 1 3]` answers
      `#[1 3]`. A tag written before a literal puts the value under it,
      `::Box{:width 2}`, a number, a boolean, null or a path touching it
      by `#`, `::Width#5`: a value is a pair of its tag and its payload,
      which `payload` reads, `::Box{:width 2} | payload` answering
      `{:width 2}`, and a tag the value already answers changes nothing,
      `::number#5` being `5`. JSON is a literal too and pastes into a
      query as it is, its keys read as keywords: `{"tags": [1, 2]}`
      answers `{:tags [1 2]}`. A keyword is not its string:
      `:id | eq "id"` answers `false`, and `toString` and `toKeyword` turn
      one into the other, `:id | toString` answering `"id"`.

      3. Reading parts. A path, ::path, names a part of a value: `/id` a
      key, `/0` an element, `/-1` the last, `/user/tags/0` a part at
      depth. A path is a value and does nothing by itself; the verb that
      takes it reads it. `get` reads it and refuses a part that is not
      there: `{:scores [10 20]} | get /scores/-1` answers `20`. `query`
      reads it softly and answers null where the part is missing, or the
      default it is given: `{:id 7} | query /age 0` answers `0`.
      `has /age` asks whether the part is there, and `it` is the value
      itself.

      4. Commands. A command is a verb and what it takes, separated by
      spaces: `add 1`, `split ","`, `take 2`, `get /price`. The verb is
      found through the kind of the subject: `count` counts the elements
      of a vector and the characters of a string. Every modifier is
      computed once, at the call, against the subject of the step, and
      the verb receives the values: `10 | add 3` answers `13`. The
      subject of a step is what the step before it answered, so in
      `{:price 4 :qty 3} | get /price | mul (get /qty)` the `/qty` is
      read from the price, a number with no parts, and the call is
      refused, `::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError`: two values of one
      record are read in one step, `get [/price /qty]`, or combined by a
      verb that reads them, `{:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty`
      answering `12`. A modifier of more than one word stands in
      parentheses: `[1 2 3] | take (count | sub 1)` answers `[1 2]`.

      5. Code. A quote, `~(…)`, is code, and a quote computes to itself,
      so the verb receives it unrun and runs it where it needs. `map`
      runs its code on each element, that element its subject:
      `[1 2 3] | map ~(mul 2)` answers `[2 4 6]`. A path is code too, the
      code that reads it from each element: `[{:name "Ann"}] | map /name`
      answers `["Ann"]`. `filter` keeps the elements its code
      holds of, `[1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1)` answering `[2 3]`; `groupBy`
      files the elements under what its code answers, or under
      themselves, `["yes" "no" "yes"] | groupBy | map ~(count)` answering
      `{:yes 2 :no 1}`; `reduce` folds the elements into a seed, calling
      its verb with the value so far as the subject and the element as
      its first arg, `[1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(add)` answering `6`, and
      running any other code on the pair of the two,
      `[{:qty 1} {:qty 2}] | reduce 0 ~(sumOf /0 /1/qty)` answering `3`.
      Several steps stand in one quote,
      `[{:price 3}] | map ~(get /price | mul 2)` answering `[6]`, and
      `apply` runs its code on the subject itself,
      `5 | apply ~(add 1 | mul 2)` answering `12`. Without the quote, a
      group is a modifier computed at the call: in
      `[{:qty 1}] | map (get /qty)` the `/qty` is read from the vector
      itself, not from each element, and `map` refuses what it is handed,
      `::vec/map/CodeKindNotTakenError`.

      6. Building answers. An answer of a new shape is built by `get`
      over a template, a literal of paths and quotes, each path read and
      each quote run against the subject:
      `{:first "Ann" :age 30} | get {:who /first :next ~(get /age | add 1)}`
      answers `{:who "Ann" :next 31}`, and `{:id 7 :qty 2} | get [/qty /id]`
      answers `[2 7]`; `query` reads the paths of a template softly. A
      group or a name written inside a literal is refused, and a value
      computed goes under a tag by `tag`, `1 | add 2 | tag ::Box`.

      7. Names. A step sees only the value handed to it: after
      `get /qty |` the subject is a number, and the record it came from
      is gone. `with` names the parts of its subject for the code of one
      step, which reads each by its bare word:
      `{:qty 2 :price 3} | with [/qty /price] ~(qty | mul price)`
      answers `6`. A name the top of a query declares is a word: a kind,
      a verb or a node under a noun. Code declared under the label of the
      values it serves is a verb the name calls,
      `:number/double ~(mul 2) | 5 | double` answering `10`, and `~double`
      hands it on as code; in the reader's own queries the verb shadows a
      verb of that name on a broader label, `::any` among them, and no
      verb of the root sees it under a label of the root. A value named
      at the top, `:rate 2`, is refused as `::bind/ValueAtTheTopError`: a
      value goes on in the subject, several in the record `get` builds,
      and code holds names of its own. A name is declared once in a
      scope, and the names a group or a quote declares stay in it.

      8. Elements and order. `count`, `sum`, `avg`, `min`, `max`,
      `first` and `last` read the elements of a vector, a set or a map.
      A map's elements are its values, the keys travelling with them:
      `{:tea 1 :jam 5} | filter ~(gt 2)` answers `{:jam 5}`, and
      `{:tea 1 :jam 5} | map ~(add 1)` answers `{:tea 2 :jam 6}`. `keys`
      answers its keys, and `entries` the pairs `[key value]`, which
      `toMap` builds back into a map. One order ranks every value, so
      anything sorts: null, then booleans, numbers, strings, keywords,
      vectors, sets, maps. `desc` turns a key to descend:
      `sort ~(get [~(get /age | desc) /name])` ranks by age descending,
      then by name.

      9. Conditions. A condition is a boolean, and nothing else is
      truthy: `filter` and `if` refuse any other value. `if` takes the
      condition as a modifier and its two branches as code:
      `5 | if (gt 0) ~("pos") ~("neg")` answers `"pos"`. Conditions of
      one value hold together by `allOf` and `anyOf`, whose modifiers
      are computed at the call, every one of them, so a condition that
      may fail where an earlier one does not hold is written as a quote
      that answers it in its turn:
      `filter ~(allOf (get /email | ne null) ~(get /email | endsWith ".org"))`.

      10. Text. A string splits into a vector and joins back,
      `"a,b,c" | split "," | join "-"` answering `"a-b-c"`; `lines`
      breaks text into its lines, and `trim`, `lower`, `upper`,
      `contains`, `startsWith` and `endsWith` read it. A string is never
      read as a number behind the reader's back: `toNumber` reads one,
      `"42" | toNumber | add 1` answering `43`. A regex, `#"…"`, is
      written as the engine reads it, no backslash doubled, and reads
      text: `"took 12ms" | match #"(\d+)ms" | get /1` answers `"12"`,
      and `capture` answers the groups it names as a record.

      11. Failures. A step that fails answers a failure, a value under
      the tag of what refused: `"x" | add 1 !| tag` answers
      `::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError`. `|` passes a failure by, and
      `!|` alone catches it, its step running on the error at rest, a
      value like any other: `"x" | add 1 !| 0` answers `0`. No value
      holds a failure, so a template whose quote fails answers the
      failure, while an error at rest, written `!{:code 404}`, sits anywhere
      and `raise` raises it. A failure carries its facts and its trace,
      a frame for each step it passed, the subject the step met and the
      steps from the one that failed, `::frame["x" ~(add 1)]`, and prints
      as an alert, the concentrate of its facts: each fact whole within
      three hundred characters and past them as the ::cell its host keeps
      it in, beside its tag, its views and its parts, while `!|` reads each fact
      whole, `!| subject | found | take 3`. `!| doc` answers the page of what refused, the page saying how to
      repair the call.

      12. Asking. `::vec | manifest` answers what lies under a kind, its
      verbs, its refusals and the roles of its chain, and `map` asks
      several kinds at once, each beside its own:
      ::snippet~([::vec ::map ::string ::number ::any] | map ~(get [~(it) ~(manifest)]))
      lists the verbs most queries use. `::vec/sort | doc` answers the
      page of a verb, and `::vec/sort | laws` the laws of a verb by
      name, each a page and a query that answers true; `tag | doc`
      answers the page of the kind of any value, and `legend` its print
      with the gloss of each part, a tag name the parts of its kind.
      An answer prints within the budget of every answer, and whole in the
      REPL of the command line; one past it prints as
      ::qlang/AnswerBeyondBudgetError, its answer the ::cell that keeps
      it, whose `answer` reads it back whole.

      13. A task. Over
      `{"users": [{"name": "Ann", "age": 30, "tags": ["new", "vip"]}, {"name": "Bo", "age": 15, "tags": []}]}`,
      `get /users | filter ~(get /age | gte 18) | map ~(get {:name /name :count ~(get /tags | count)})`
      answers `[{:name "Ann" :count 2}]`. Look at the data before shaping
      it, `shape` its structure, the kind at each of its parts, `keys`,
      `get /0`, `tag`, `count`, and build the query a step at a time,
      running each.

      14. The chapters, in the order they rest on each other:
      ::link~(::qlang/values) values, kinds and tags;
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline) the steps of a query;
      ::link~(::qlang/names) names and their records;
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs) the verbs that live on kinds;
      ::link~(::qlang/errors) the failure as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang/code) code as a value;
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext) the pages and how to move through
      them;
      ::link~(::qlang/hosts) the tool the language runs in and the
      budget of an answer.
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts |~~ No value: `::qlang` names the topic of the language. ~~| #[]
    | throws :AnswerBeyondBudgetError
        |~~ A host printed an answer past the budget of every answer, the
            characters of its print `:budget` names, and prints this
            instead of it: `:characters` tells the characters of the print
            and `:answer` holds the answer, which the alert shows as the
            cell where the host keeps it, whose `answer` reads it back
            whole, `::cell"…" | answer | take 3`, beside its tag, the
            views its kind reveals and its parts, `:answer/count` and
            `:answer/addend/found`, each key the chain that reads it.
            Narrow the answer by `get`, `take`, `keys`, `count` or
            `shape`, or, where the host has streams, write it whole through
            the door the query opens itself: `out` a text, `print | out` the
            literal of a value and `json | out` its JSON. No step raises it
            and no `!|` reaches it.
            ::link~(::qlang/hosts) ::link~(::cell)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :characters |~~ The characters of the print of the answer. ~~| ::number
          | wires :budget |~~ The budget every host prints an answer within. ~~| ::number
          | wires :answer |~~ The answer, which the alert shows as the cell its host keeps it in. ~~|)
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::qlang` names the topic of the language, its page and its
            chapters, and takes no payload: it is asked by itself,
            `::qlang | doc :values`.
        ~~|
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAValue
        |~~ No value is put under `::qlang`. ~~|
        ~(1 | tag ::qlang !| tag | eq ::qlang/PayloadKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :runsAGroup
        |~~ A group runs its steps on the value in the pipe. ~~|
        ~(5 | (add 1 | mul 2) | eq 12)
    | proves :buildsAShapeByATemplate
        |~~ A template of `get` builds a shape from the value, its paths
            read and its quotes run on it.
        ~~|
        ~({:first "Ann" :age 30} | get {:who /first :next ~(get /age | add 1)} | eq {:who "Ann" :next 31})
    | proves :buildsTheShapeOfEachElement
        |~~ Under `map` a template builds the shape of each element. ~~|
        ~([{:id 1 :tags [:new :vip]}] | map ~(get {:id /id :count ~(get /tags | count)}) | eq [{:id 1 :count 2}])
    | proves :walksAPath
        |~~ A path walks keys and indices, `-1` from the end. ~~|
        ~({:user {:scores [10 20]}} | get /user/scores/-1 | eq 20)
    | proves :readsAQuotedKey
        |~~ A key with a space is read quoted. ~~|
        ~({"first name": "Ann"} | get /"first name" | eq "Ann")
    | proves :readsATemplateSoftly
        |~~ `query` reads the paths of a template softly. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann"} | query [~(get /name) /age] | eq ["Ann" null])
    | proves :readsSoftly
        |~~ `query` reads a missing key as null. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann"} | query /age | eq null)
    | proves :fallsBack
        |~~ `coalesce` answers a fallback for null. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann"} | query /age | coalesce 0 | eq 0)
    | proves :multipliesTwoFields
        |~~ Two fields of one record multiply by `mulOf`. ~~|
        ~({:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 12)
    | proves :readsAModifierFromTheSubjectOfItsStep
        |~~ After `get /price |` a modifier is read from the price. ~~|
        ~({:price 4 :qty 3} | get /price | mul (get /qty) !| tag | eq ::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError)
    | proves :subtractsTwoFields
        |~~ Two fields of one record subtract by `subOf`. ~~|
        ~({:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total /paid | eq 7)
    | proves :runsAPipelineInATemplate
        |~~ A pipeline in a template stands in a quote. ~~|
        ~({:age 30} | get [~(get /age | add 1) /age] | eq [31 30])
    | proves :losesTheRecordAfterAStep
        |~~ After `get /qty |` a modifier reads the number, not the record. ~~|
        ~({:qty 2 :price 3} | get /qty | mul (get /price) !| tag | eq ::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError)
    | proves :takesCodeAsAQuote
        |~~ Code a verb runs on each element is a quote. ~~|
        ~([{:age 3} {:age 20}] | filter ~(get /age | gte 18) | count | eq 1)
    | proves :quotesOneWord
        |~~ `~name` quotes one word. ~~|
        ~("[3 1 2] | map ~toString | first" | parse | answer | eq "3")
    | proves :refusesAValueForCode
        |~~ A value computed at the call is refused where code is wanted.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | filter (gt 1) !| tag | eq ::vec/filter/PredicateKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :splitsAndJoins
        |~~ A string splits into a vector and joins back. ~~|
        ~("a,b,c" | split "," | join "-" | eq "a-b-c")
    | proves :cutsAString
        |~~ A string counts, drops and takes by characters. ~~|
        ~("db_host" | get [~(count) ~(drop 3) ~(take 2)] | eq [7 "host" "db"])
    | proves :countsOccurrences
        |~~ `groupBy | map ~count` counts each value. ~~|
        ~(["yes" "no" "yes"] | groupBy | map ~count | eq {:yes 2 :no 1})
    | proves :turnsKeysIntoData
        |~~ Entries turn the keys of a map into data. ~~|
        ~({:auth {:port 3000}} | entries | map ~(unionOf /1 (get {:name ~(get /0 | toString)})) | eq [{:port 3000 :name "auth"}])
    | proves :buildsAMapFromEntries
        |~~ Entries build back into the map. ~~|
        ~({:id 7} | entries | toMap | eq {:id 7})
    | proves :folds
        |~~ `reduce` folds the elements from a seed. ~~|
        ~([3 1 2] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 6)
    | proves :foldsCodeOnThePair
        |~~ Any other code folds on the pair of the value so far and the
            element.
        ~~|
        ~([{:qty 1} {:qty 2}] | reduce 0 ~(sumOf /0 /1/qty) | eq 3)
    | proves :ordersASet
        |~~ A set stands in the one order. ~~|
        ~("#[:tea :cake :jam] | first" | parse | answer | eq :cake)
    | proves :answersASetByToSet
        |~~ `toSet` answers a set. ~~|
        ~([2 3 2 1] | toSet | eq #[1 2 3])
    | proves :answersASetByKeys
        |~~ `keys` answers a set. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann" :age 30} | keys | eq #[:age :name])
    | proves :reversesASetIntoAVector
        |~~ `reverse` of a set answers a vector. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea :cake :jam] | reverse | eq [:tea :jam :cake])
    | proves :readsTheValuesOfAMap
        |~~ A map's elements are its values, its keys travelling with
            them.
        ~~|
        ~({:tea 1 :jam 5} | filter ~(gt 2) | eq {:jam 5})
    | proves :branchesOnABoolean
        |~~ `if` branches on a boolean. ~~|
        ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("pos") ~("neg") | eq "pos")
    | proves :refusesTruthiness
        |~~ Nothing is truthy: a number is no condition. ~~|
        ~(5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no") !| tag | eq ::any/if/ConditionKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :turnsAStringIntoAKeyword
        |~~ `toKeyword` turns a string into a keyword. ~~|
        ~("draft" | toKeyword | eq :draft)
    | proves :turnsAKeywordIntoAString
        |~~ `toString` turns a keyword into its string. ~~|
        ~(:draft | toString | eq "draft")
    | proves :printsText
        |~~ `print` answers the text a value reads back from. ~~|
        ~(7 | print | eq "7")
    | proves :catchesAFailureToItsFacts
        |~~ `!|` catches a failure, its step running on the error at rest,
            which holds its facts.
        ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| subject | found | tag | eq ::string)
    | proves :refusesUnderTheInput
        |~~ An input of none of its kinds is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(1 | add "x" !| tag | eq ::number/add/AddendKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :writesAnError
        |~~ An error is written as a literal, a value whose facts read as
            fields.
        ~~|
        ~(!{:reason :late} | get /reason | eq :late)
    | proves :raisesAMap
        |~~ `raise` builds a failure from a map. ~~|
        ~({:reason :late} | raise !| get [~(tag) /reason] | eq [::error :late])
    | proves :writesAnErrorOfItsOwnKind
        |~~ A tag before the bang names the kind of the error. ~~|
        ~(::Late!{:by 3} | get [~(tag) /by] | eq [::Late 3])
    | proves :asksWhetherTheStepBeforeStood
        |~~ `ok !| false` asks whether the step before stood. ~~|
        ~(get [~(!{:code 404} | raise | ok !| false) ~(5 | ok !| false)] | eq [false true])
    | proves :holdsTheTrace
        |~~ The trace holds the steps a failure skipped. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | last | skipped | eq ~(mul 2))
    | proves :runsAQuote
        |~~ `apply` runs a quote on a subject. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(add 1 | mul 2) | eq 12)
    | proves :parsesText
        |~~ `parse` turns text into a quote. ~~|
        ~("add 1" | parse | eq ~(add 1))
    | proves :declaresAKindOfYourOwn
        |~~ A kind of your own takes a verb and an input held to its
            kinds.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A parcel. ~~| | :Box/put (::fn | takes :weight |~~ A weight. ~~| /0 ::number | impl ~(within ~(append weight))) | ::Box[1] | get [~(put 7) ~(put "x" !| tag)] | eq [::Box[1 7] ::Box/put/WeightKindNotTakenError])
    | proves :givesAnInputADefault
        |~~ An input read by `query` has a default. ~~|
        ~(:number/plus (::fn | takes :by |~~ How much, one where none. ~~| ~(query /0 1) | impl ~(add by)) | 5 | get [~(plus) ~(plus 2)] | eq [6 7])
    | proves :runsANamedQuote
        |~~ A quote held in a record runs by `apply` under the name `with`
            gives it.
        ~~|
        ~({:shipping ~(add 5)} | with [/shipping] ~(20 | apply shipping) | eq 25)
    | proves :readsAStepAsACall
        |~~ The step of `add 1` is a `::call` named `add`. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | first | get [~(tag) /name] | eq [::call :add])
    | proves :readsADoc
        |~~ A doc splits into its prose and the code it holds. ~~|
        ~(|~~ a ~(eq 1) b ~~| | segments | count | eq 3)
    | proves :looksAtTheKeysOfTheData
        |~~ The keys of the first element show the shape of the data. ~~|
        ~([{:id 1 :tags ["a"]}] | get /0 | keys | eq #[:id :tags])
    | proves :looksAtTheShapeOfTheData
        |~~ The shape of the data shows the kinds its records hold. ~~|
        ~({:users [{:age 30} {:age "x"}]} | shape | eq ::shape{:users [{:age #[::number ::string]}]})
    | proves :opensTheChapters
        |~~ The links of the first page open its chapters. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | doc | links | map ~open | count | gt 5)
    | tells :values
        |~~ Values. Everything qlang handles is a value, data, code, prose
            and failures alike, so everything the language says about
            itself is a value the pipeline reads. A value never changes,
            and each has a kind, the name of which is a tag: it tells how
            to read the value it stands on, and the tag taken as a value,
            `::Box`, is the kind itself, which a query asks for its page
            and its verbs. Rests on nothing; the pipeline, the verbs and
            the hypertext rest on it.
            ::snippet~(kinds) lists the kinds the scope reads.
            ::link~(::tag)
            ::link~(::qlang/pipeline)

            A literal implies its kind: it opens with the sign of the
            kind and closes with its pair, and everything between is
            literal, so a literal reads neither the scope nor the value in
            the pipe, and a group or a name written inside one is refused.
            A tag written before a literal puts the value under the tag,
            the literal touching it by its own sign, `::Box{:width 2}`,
            `::Point[1 2]`, `::Name"Ann"`, `::Status:active`,
            `::Rule~(add 1)`, and a literal whose first character would
            continue the tag's name, a number, `true`, `false`, `null` or
            a path, by `#`, `::Width#5`, `::Field#/width`; the kind
            attaches the tag to the payload or refuses it, saying why the
            payload does not fit, and builds no other value, but for a
            kind of the core, whose constructor reads its payload as a
            value of its own, `::set[2 1]` answering `#[1 2]`. `tag` puts
            the value in the pipe under a tag, a value computed among
            them, `1 | add 2 | tag ::Box`; tags stack and read from the
            outside in, `::Gift::Box[1]`, and `payload` takes one off. A
            step answers a new value and the old one stays as it was. Every
            value prints as a literal that reads back as itself. A value is
            told by how its print opens: `"` a string, `:` a keyword, `::`
            and a name alone a tag and with a payload touching it a value
            under that tag, `[` a vector, `#[` a set, `#"` a regex, `{` a
            map, `/` a path, `~(` a quote, `|~~` a doc, `!{` an error at
            rest, a digit or a minus a number, and `true`, `false` and
            `null` themselves; a failure a host prints is its error at
            rest under the line `|~| raised, and no !| caught it`. A kind
            of the core reads a payload of its own kind under its tag as
            the value itself, `::set[2 1]` printing `#[1 2]`.
        ~~|
        #[::tag ::null ::boolean ::number ::string ::regex ::keyword ::path ::vec ::map ::set ::desc ::tagged]
    | proves :values/impliesTheKinds
        |~~ Each literal answers its kind. ~~|
        ~([42 "Ann" :active [1] {:id 7} #[1] true null] | map ~tag | eq [::number ::string ::keyword ::vec ::map ::set ::boolean ::null])
    | proves :values/putsAnyPayloadUnderATag
        |~~ A literal touches its tag by its own sign, and a number, a
            boolean, null or a path by `#`.
        ~~|
        ~([::Box{:width 2} ::Box[1 2] ::Box"tea" ::Box:fragile ::Box#3 ::Box#true ::Box#null ::Box#/width]
          | map ~payload
          | eq [{:width 2} [1 2] "tea" :fragile 3 true null /width])
    | proves :values/attachesTheTagOrRefusesThePayload
        |~~ A kind attaches its tag to a payload or refuses it. ~~|
        ~(:Pos |~~ A number above zero. ~~| (::kind | holds :NotPositiveError |~~ Above zero. ~~| ~(gt 0))
          | get [~(::Pos#3 | payload) ~(::Pos#-3 !| tag)]
          | eq [3 ::Pos/NotPositiveError])
    | proves :values/computesNothingInsideALiteral
        |~~ A group or a name inside a literal is refused, and a value
            computed goes under a tag by `tag`.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~("[(1 | add 2)]" | parse !| tag) ~(":rate 2 | [rate]" | parse !| tag) ~(1 | add 2 | tag ::Box | payload)]
          | eq [::quote/ParseError ::quote/ParseError 3])
    | proves :values/isToldByHowItsPrintOpens
        |~~ A string, a keyword, a tag, a vector, a set, a regex, a map,
            a quote and an error at rest each print as their kind opens.
        ~~|
        ~(["tea" :tea ::Tea [1] #[1] #"tea" {:tea 1} ~(tea) !{:tea 1}] | map ~tag | eq [::string ::keyword ::tag ::vec ::set ::regex ::map ::quote ::error])
    | proves :values/readsAKindOfTheCoreUnderItsTag
        |~~ A kind of the core reads its payload under its tag as the
            value itself.
        ~~|
        ~([::set[2 1] ::number#5 ::map{:id 7}] | eq [#[1 2] 5 {:id 7}])
    | proves :values/stacksTags
        |~~ Tags stack and read from the outside in. ~~|
        ~([7 8] | tag ::Inner | tag ::Outer | get [~(tag) ~(payload | tag) ~(payload | payload)] | eq [::Outer ::Inner [7 8]])
    | proves :values/readsTheOuterTag
        |~~ `tag` reads the outer tag. ~~|
        ~(::Box::Crate{:width 2} | tag | eq ::Box)
    | proves :values/leavesTheOldValueAsItWas
        |~~ Appending leaves the old vector as it was. ~~|
        ~(["tea" "milk"] | get [~(it) ~(append "bread")] | eq [["tea" "milk"] ["tea" "milk" "bread"]])
    | proves :values/readsBackItsPrint
        |~~ A value reads back from its print. ~~|
        ~({:order 7 :items ["tea" "milk"] :flags #[:paid] :box ::Box[3]} | eq (print | parse | answer))
    | tells :pipeline
        |~~ The pipeline. A query is a subject and its steps, each a
            command on the value the step before it answered, and the
            answer is the last value; the shape of an answer is written as
            the literal it takes, so a query names what it reads. Rests on
            values; the verbs are its commands, and code holds its steps
            as a value.
            ::link~(::path) ::link~(::group)
            ::link~(::vec/map) ::link~(::qlang/names)

            `|` runs the next step on the answer of the one before. A step
            is a command, and a value stands at the head of a query, of a
            group and of a quote, and after a declaration, where it is the
            subject, or as what a verb takes; a value written as a later
            step is refused, and a value goes under a tag by `tag`. `map`
            runs a quote or a path on each element of a vector or each
            value of a map. A pipe written before `!|` stands for it. A
            path, `/name`, `/0`, `/-1`, `/user/name`, is read by `get`, and
            a miss is an error there; `query` reads it softly, and `put`,
            `update` and `delete` write it. A modifier is computed against
            the subject of its own step, the value the step before it
            answered, so two fields of one record are read in one step, by
            the template of `get` or by the names `with` gives the code of
            one step, and steps in parentheses run on the value in the
            pipe and answer what their last step answers. A line break is
            a space, so a step on the next line joins by `|`, written at
            the end of a line or at the start of the next, and around a
            declaration it tells where the declaration ends: a keyword
            that begins a line with a doc or a step after it opens a
            declaration of its own, and the body of a name with a path
            begun on the line after the name runs on through the steps
            joined to it up to the next declaration or a value written
            after `|`, so a body of one step is written on the line of its
            name. A comment,
            `|~ … ~|`, nesting, or `|~|` to the end of its line, is
            whitespace too. `|` and `!|` bind alike, from left to right.
            A group is a fork: it starts from the value and the names
            outside it, and only its answer leaves it; a literal computes
            nothing, and the quotes of a template `get` runs are forks of
            their own, so they see nothing of each other.
        ~~|
        #[::path ::group ::bind ::call ::construct]
    | proves :pipeline/runsStepAfterStep
        |~~ Five plus one, times two, is twelve. ~~|
        ~(5 | add 1 | mul 2 | eq 12)
    | proves :pipeline/refusesAValueAsALaterStep
        |~~ A value written as a later step is refused. ~~|
        ~("5 | [1 2]" | parse !| tag | eq ::quote/ParseError)
    | proves :pipeline/putsAValueUnderATagByTag
        |~~ A value goes under a tag by `tag`. ~~|
        ~([7 8] | tag ::Box | tag | eq ::Box)
    | proves :pipeline/takesAPipeBeforeTheFailTrack
        |~~ A pipe before `!|` stands for it. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | !| 0 | eq 0)
    | proves :pipeline/readsAPath
        |~~ A path reads a nested part. ~~|
        ~({:user {:name "Ann"}} | get /user/name | eq "Ann")
    | proves :pipeline/failsOnAMiss
        |~~ A miss is an error. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann"} | get /nmae !| tag | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :pipeline/namesTwoPartsForTheCodeOfAStep
        |~~ `with` names two parts of the record for the code of one step. ~~|
        ~({:qty 2 :price 3} | with [/qty /price] ~(qty | mul price) | eq 6)
    | proves :pipeline/holdsTheValueBesideWhatItComputes
        |~~ A template holds the value beside what its quotes compute. ~~|
        ~(5 | get [~(it) ~(add 1)] | eq [5 6])
    | proves :pipeline/joinsLinesByAPipe
        |~~ Steps on several lines join by `|`, a comment between them. ~~|
        ~("[3 1 2]\n|~| the order\n| sort\n| first" | parse | answer | eq 1)
    | proves :pipeline/joinsNoStepsByALine
        |~~ A line break is a space, and joins no steps. ~~|
        ~("[3 1 2]\nsort" | parse !| tag | eq ::quote/ParseError)
    | proves :pipeline/runsADefinitionBegunOnTheNextLineOn
        |~~ The body of a name with a path begun on the line after it runs
            on through the steps joined to it, and the subject passes on.
        ~~|
        ~("[1 2 3] | :vec/second\n  ~(get /1) | count" | parse | answer | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :pipeline/nestsComments
        |~~ Comments nest and stand where spaces stand. ~~|
        ~("|~ outer |~ inner ~| ~| [1 2] |~ a note ~| | count" | parse | answer | eq 2)
    | proves :pipeline/seesNothingOfASibling
        |~~ One quote of a template sees nothing of another. ~~|
        ~(get [~(:bonus 1 | bonus) ~(bonus)] !| tag | eq ::call/UnknownNameError)
    | tells :names
        |~~ Names. A name holds what its declaration answered for the
            steps after it: `:rate 0.2` declares it in the scope where it
            stands. The top of a query, of a source and of a cell declares
            the words its steps share, a kind, a verb and a node under a
            noun, and a value under a bare name is refused there,
            `::bind/ValueAtTheTopError`; code holds names of its own, the
            names `with` gives its body among them. A name with
            a path declares a node under the noun its path names before
            its last name, `:Box/area` under `::Box`, a noun declared
            before it, and the tag of a name is its address, `::Box/area`.
            A name is a record, `::binding`, with its page, its source,
            its value and the source it came from, so where a thing was
            declared is read as data. The scope of a step is what the
            steps before it wrote; a step inside `(…)` or a quote a verb
            runs writes a scope that closes with it. Under every scope
            lies the root, which the host's sources declared as cells
            before the first step of a reader, the language's first; a
            query, a cell of a session and a source of the root are
            written alike. Rests on values and the pipeline; the verbs are
            names that run. ::link~(::binding) ::link~(::bind)
            ::link~(::qlang/verbs)

            `env` lists what the reader declared, `kinds` the kinds the
            scope reads, and what lies under a noun `manifest` lists. A
            name reads its record by `binding`. A name declared inside a
            group closes with it, and which names a quote reads the
            chapter of code tells. A name declared under a label resides
            on it: it serves the values of the label, found by its name
            through the walk of their tags, and refuses any other subject
            under its address; a quote taken from one label and declared
            under another resides on the second. A name is declared once
            in a scope and again in a scope inside it, which shadows it
            for the steps after; a name declared in the code stands over a
            verb of its name, the verb called by its address there,
            `[1 2] | vec/count`, and a name that holds a value is read where
            a value stands, a later step refusing it as
            `::call/ValueAsALaterStepError`; an
            address the root holds is declared once at all, so a reader
            extends a noun of the root and replaces none of its nodes. A
            verb the reader declares under a label shadows a verb of its
            name on a broader label in the reader's own calls, since the
            walk of a subject's tags reaches the narrower first; under a
            label of the root no verb of the root sees it, a verb of the
            root running in the root's scope, where a name under a noun of
            the root reads the root alone, while a verb under a label the
            reader declared goes where the label's values go. A name
            begins with a letter of any script or `_`. The docs in the
            slot of a declaration, between its name and its body, are its
            page, one doc each. A name under a label resolves its names
            through the tree of names as it stands at the call, itself
            among them, so it recurses by name, and a runaway meets the
            limit of the evaluation depth.
        ~~|
        #[::binding]
    | proves :names/listsUnderANoun
        |~~ `manifest` lists what lies under a noun. ~~|
        ~(::vec | manifest | has ::vec/count)
    | proves :names/closesWithItsGroup
        |~~ A name declared in a group closes with it. ~~|
        ~((:Crate |~~ A crate. ~~| | ::Crate | doc | content) | get [~(it) ~(env | has :Crate)] | eq ["A crate." false])
    | proves :names/servesTheValuesOfItsLabel
        |~~ A name under a label serves its values. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/area ~(mulOf /width /height) | ::Box{:width 3 :height 2} | area | eq 6)
    | proves :names/extendsANounOfTheLanguage
        |~~ A reader's name under `vec` serves vectors. ~~|
        ~(:vec/second ~(get /1) | [7 8 9] | second | eq 8)
    | proves :names/refusesAnotherSubject
        |~~ Another subject is refused under the name's address. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/area ~(mulOf /width /height) | {:width 3 :height 2} | Box/area !| tag | eq ::Box/area/SubjectNotBoxError)
    | proves :names/residesWhereDeclared
        |~~ A quote taken from one label resides on the one it is declared
            under.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/label ~("box") | :Crate |~~ A crate. ~~| | :Crate/label (::Box/label | binding | value) | get [~(::Crate{} | label) ~(::Box{} | Crate/label !| tag)] | eq ["box" ::Crate/label/SubjectNotCrateError])
    | proves :names/needsItsNoun
        |~~ A name under no declared noun is refused. ~~|
        ~(:Box/area ~(1) !| tag | eq ::bind/NounNotDeclaredError)
    | proves :names/declaresOnce
        |~~ A name is declared once in a scope. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box |~~ A crate. ~~| !| tag | eq ::bind/NameDeclaredTwiceError)
    | proves :names/standsOverAVerbOfItsName
        |~~ A name `with` gives stands over the verb of its name, which
            its address calls, and is refused as a later step.
        ~~|
        ~(::order{:id 100 :item :bigmac :count 2} | with [/count] ~(get [~(count) ~(map/count) ~(keys | count !| tag)]) | eq [2 3 ::call/ValueAsALaterStepError])
    | proves :names/shadowsForTheReaderAlone
        |~~ A reader's verb shadows a broader one in the reader's call,
            and a verb of the root never sees it.
        ~~|
        ~(:vec/doc ~("mine") | [1 2] | get [~(doc) ~(tutorial | first | tag)] | eq ["mine" ::doc])
    | proves :names/beginsWithALetter
        |~~ A name begins with a letter or `_`, and a digit begins none. ~~|
        ~("1x" | parse !| tag | eq ::quote/ParseError)
    | proves :names/holdsEachDocOfItsSlot
        |~~ Two docs in the slot are two parts of its page. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| |~~ It holds parcels. ~~| | ::Box | binding | docs | map ~content | eq ["A box." "It holds parcels."])
    | proves :names/recursesByName
        |~~ A name calls itself through the tree of names. ~~|
        ~(:map/walk ~(get {:name /name :kids ~(get /kids | map ~walk)}) | {:name "Ann" :kids [{:name "Bob" :kids []}]} | walk | get /kids/0/name | eq "Bob")
    | tells :verbs
        |~~ Verbs. A verb lives on a kind, the one whose values it serves,
            and has an address, `::vec/sort`; so what can be done with a
            value is asked of its kind, and one name serves many kinds.
            The verbs of the kinds the walk of a value's tags reaches and
            those of every value are the whole of its verbs: a verb in none
            of them does not exist. Rests on values and the pipeline;
            errors name the verb that refused.
            ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) lists the verbs of a kind and
            ::snippet~(::any | manifest) those of every value.
            ::link~(::qlang/errors)

            A name finds its verb through the kind of its subject: its
            tags from the outside in, `::tagged` after each, then the
            kind of the core beneath them, then the verbs every value
            answers, on `::any`; a verb of vectors
            reaches a vector under a tag, and an answer of the subject's
            kind keeps the tag. A verb is a
            function named under a label, built from `::fn` by a chain of
            commands with its recipe last,
            `:number/plus (::fn | takes :by |~~ How much. ~~| /0 ::number | impl ~(add by))`:
            `on` names its subject, `takes` an input with its page, its
            selector over the args and its kinds, `returns` its
            answer, `throws` a refusal and the kind of its facts,
            `holds` an invariant and `proves` a law, and its call reads
            them from the function, each input bound by its name in the
            recipe, a name the recipe declares no second time; `manifest`
            answers them before a call. A recipe written alone under a
            label, `:number/double ~(mul 2)`, is the function of no
            commands it is, which takes no args; an input, an answer, a
            subject and an invariant are written in the chain alone. An
            answer of the verb's own label goes back under the tags the
            subject came through, and a verb that combines the subject
            with a value of its kind, `add`, `append`, `concat` or
            `union`, answers beneath the tags the two share, a bare one
            sharing any, two under different tags combining in no step. A
            modifier is evaluated at the call
            against the subject, the value in the pipe, which is always
            the subject of the call; a verb that combines several values
            of one subject reads each from its args, `mulOf /price /qty`;
            an input that runs code later takes
            a quote, `~name` or `~(get /path)` the quote of one step, and
            a condition is a boolean. A label is a kind declared under a
            name, by the chain that builds it from `::kind`,
            `:Box |~~ A parcel. ~~| (::kind | wires :width |~~ The width. ~~| /width ::number)`,
            or by its page alone, a kind of no commands: `accepts` names its
            payload and its kinds, `wires` a field the payload holds, its
            selector and its kinds, `reveals` a view of its values, a verb of
            the kind whose answer a host shows beside a fact past its room,
            `holds` an invariant of the value or of a field, `throws` a
            refusal and the kind of its facts, and `proves` a law,
            and a kind of the core `impl` its constructor, which its host
            hands; the kind attaches the tag to the payload or refuses
            it, and builds no other value, so every value put under it
            keeps them. A tag no declaration answers is a kind of
            identity, which checks nothing and lays the tag on the value.
            A verb answers under the kind its `returns` names. A verb of a
            noun itself, whose subject `on` names `::tag`, takes the tag
            name as its subject, so a noun that stands for a thing outside
            the language is addressed bare, `::Jenkins | builds`, while a
            verb of the noun's values passes the tag name to the verb of
            its name on `::any`, `::Box | print` printing `::Box` where
            `:Box/print` prints the values of `::Box`, and refuses it
            where none lives there. A verb is told
            as a story, in one order: the verb and its page, its inputs,
            its answer, its refusals, its laws and its recipe, one chain; a
            kind as its page, its payload, its fields, its views, its
            refusals, its invariants, its laws and its constructor; and the refusal of a
            step as its page, which repairs the step, its facts and its
            laws. `source` reads the story of a name as it is written,
            `::vec/sort | source`.
        ~~|
        #[::any ::fn ::invariant ::kind]
    | proves :verbs/findsTheVerbOfTheKind
        |~~ `sort` finds the verb of vectors. ~~|
        ~([3 1 2] | sort | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :verbs/keepsTheTag
        |~~ A verb of vectors reaches a vector under a tag, and an answer
            of the subject's kind keeps the tag.
        ~~|
        ~(::Box[3 1 2] | sort | eq ::Box[1 2 3])
    | proves :verbs/answersTheVerbsOfTheKindsItsWalkReaches
        |~~ A set answers a verb of vectors it does not declare. ~~|
        ~(#[3 1 2] | get [~(count) ~(binding :count | name)] | eq [3 :vec/count])
    | proves :verbs/declaresTheInputs
        |~~ `query` declares its path and its default as its inputs. ~~|
        ~(::any/query | binding | value | inputs | map /name | eq [:path :default])
    | proves :verbs/answersUnderTheTags
        |~~ A sum goes back under the tag the subject came through. ~~|
        ~(::Width#2 | add 3 | eq ::Width#5)
    | proves :verbs/isAFunctionNamedUnderALabel
        |~~ A verb is a function named under a label. ~~|
        ~(:number/plus (::fn | takes :by |~~ How much. ~~| /0 ::number | impl ~(add by)) | 5 | plus 2 | eq 7)
    | proves :verbs/takesNoArgsForARecipeWrittenAlone
        |~~ A recipe written alone takes no args. ~~|
        ~(:number/double ~(mul 2) | 5 | get [~(double) ~(double 3 !| tag)] | eq [10 ::number/double/ArgsBeyondInputsError])
    | proves :verbs/combinesValuesOfOneSubject
        |~~ A verb that combines several values of one subject reads each
            from its args.
        ~~|
        ~({:price 100 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 300)
    | proves :verbs/takesAQuoteForLaterCode
        |~~ Code run later is a quote. ~~|
        ~([{:age 3} {:age 20}] | filter ~(get /age | gt 18) | count | eq 1)
    | proves :verbs/takesAPathAsCode
        |~~ A path is the code that reads it from each element,
            `sort /age`.
        ~~|
        ~("[{:age 3}] | sort /age | first | get /age" | parse | answer | eq 3)
    | proves :verbs/needsABoolean
        |~~ A condition is a boolean. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | filter ~(1) !| get [~(tag) ~(predicate | expected)] | eq [::vec/filter/ConditionKindNotTakenError #[::boolean]])
    | proves :verbs/laysAnUndeclaredTag
        |~~ A tag no declaration answers checks nothing. ~~|
        ~(::Small#12 | payload | eq 12)
    | proves :verbs/answersUnderTheKindItReturns
        |~~ A verb answers under the kind its `returns` names. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/half (::fn | returns |~~ The half. ~~| ::number | impl ~(get /0 | div 2)) | ::Box[4] | half | eq 2)
    | proves :verbs/takesTheTagOfItsNoun
        |~~ A verb of a noun itself takes the tag name as its subject. ~~|
        ~(:Desk |~~ A help desk. ~~| | :Desk/hours (::fn | on |~~ The desk itself. ~~| ::tag | impl ~("9 to 5")) | ::Desk | hours | eq "9 to 5")
    | proves :verbs/refusesTheTagForAVerbOfValues
        |~~ A verb of the noun's values refuses the tag name where no verb
            of its name lives on `::any`.
        ~~|
        ~(:Desk |~~ A help desk. ~~| | :Desk/size ~(count) | ::Desk | size !| tag | eq ::Desk/size/SubjectNotDeskError)
    | proves :verbs/passesTheTagToTheVerbOfEveryValue
        |~~ A verb of the noun's values passes the tag name to the verb of
            its name on `::any`.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/print ~("box") | get [~(::Box | print) ~(::Box[1] | print)] | eq ["::Box" "box"])
    | proves :verbs/keepsTheInvariantsOfItsKind
        |~~ A value put under a kind keeps the kind's invariants. ~~|
        ~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| (::kind | holds :NotPositiveError |~~ A number above zero. ~~| ~(gt 0)) | ::Pos#-3 !| tag | eq ::Pos/NotPositiveError)
    | tells :errors
        |~~ Errors. A failure does not throw: a step that fails answers an
            error, a value that carries the facts of the failure, the trace
            it left and the tag of the site that refused, which names the
            page of its procedure. A reader learning a tool fails often,
            and the failure is where what it needs is cheapest to give.
            Rests on values, the pipeline and verbs; the hypertext holds
            the pages the sites name.
            ::link~(::error) ::link~(::fail)
            ::link~(::trace) ::link~(::qlang/code)

            A failed step answers a failure the tag of its site names. `|`
            passes a failure by, and `map` answers the first an element
            answers. `a !| b` alone catches a failure, running `b` on the
            error at rest, a value like any other, and recovers each
            failure where it is read. No value holds a failure: a template
            whose quote fails, a literal whose constructor refuses its
            payload and a name whose body fails answer the failure. The
            facts name what refused and hold the value refused, but where
            the step refused its subject, which the first frame of the
            trace holds, and `doc` reads the page of the site. The trace is
            written as the failure flies: a frame for each level it left,
            the subject its step met and the steps from the one that
            failed, the last frame holding the steps skipped at the level
            that reads it, a quote that runs again as held code runs, and
            the frame of a verb that ran its code on a part of its subject
            the path of that part. An error
            at rest raised again resumes the trace it holds, and one that
            holds none, an empty `:trace` among them, starts it over.
            Whether the step before stood is `ok !| false`.
        ~~|
        #[::error ::fail ::frame ::trace]
    | proves :errors/namesTheSite
        |~~ The error's tag names its site. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| tag | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | proves :errors/recoversWhereRead
        |~~ `!|` inside the code `map` runs recovers each failure. ~~|
        ~([1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2 !| 0) | eq [2 0])
    | proves :errors/holdsTheRefusedSubject
        |~~ The first frame holds the subject the step refused. ~~|
        ~("a" | mul 2 !| get [~(subject | found) ~(trace | first | subject)] | eq ["a" "a"])
    | proves :errors/holdsAFrameForEachLevel
        |~~ The trace holds a frame for each level the error left. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(add "x" | mul 2) | sub 3 !| trace | map ~failed | eq [~(add "x") ~(apply ~(add "x" | mul 2))])
    | proves :errors/holdsTheSkippedSteps
        |~~ The last frame holds the steps skipped. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | last | skipped | toString | eq "mul 2")
    | proves :errors/runsTheSkippedStepsAgain
        |~~ The steps a failure skipped run again by `apply`. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | last | skipped | with {:rest ~it} ~(5 | apply rest) | eq 10)
    | proves :errors/namesThePathOfThePart
        |~~ The frame of a verb that ran its code on an element names the
            path of the element.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map ~(mul 2) !| trace | last | path | eq /1)
    | proves :errors/resumesItsTrace
        |~~ A map raised again resumes its trace. ~~|
        ~(!{:code 404} | raise | count !| raise | add 1 !| get /trace | last | skipped | eq ~(count | add 1))
    | proves :errors/asksWhetherTheStepBeforeStood
        |~~ `ok !| false` answers whether the step before stood. ~~|
        ~(get [~(!{:code 404} | raise | ok !| false) ~(42 | ok !| false)] | eq [false true])
    | tells :code
        |~~ Code. A quote, `~(…)`, holds steps unrun as a value: a verb
            takes one where it needs code, `filter`, `sort`, `if`, `apply`
            runs one on a subject, and a quote is taken apart and built
            like any vector of steps. Because code is a value, the laws of
            a name run, a page holds links that open, and the trace of an
            error holds the steps it skipped, a quote that runs again.
            Rests on values and the pipeline; the hypertext is written in
            it.
            ::link~(::quote) ::link~(::qlang/hypertext)
            ::link~(::call/ValueWithheldError)

            A quote written in a step, what a verb takes or a part of a
            template it takes, is code of that step: the step writes at its
            head the values of its scope the quote reads,
            `{:bonus 10} | with [/bonus] ~([1 2] | map ~(add bonus))` handing `map`
            the code `~(:bonus 10 | add bonus)`, so it runs as it reads by whatever road the
            call hands it on, a verb written in the language among them.
            All code runs in a lane of its own: the verbs, the nodes and
            the labels of the step that runs it and the names it declares
            itself, a value that step names withheld from it and refused
            under its name, `::call/ValueWithheldError`. Code held as
            data, a quote in a name, a field or a page, a law and a snippet
            among them, is handed on as it is written, so a value it needs
            is an input of a function, `apply f [4]`, a name `with` gives
            its body, or is written at its head by `fill`,
            `~(gt lim) | fill {:lim 4}`.
        ~~|
        #[::quote]
    | proves :code/runsByApply
        |~~ A quote is a value, which counts its steps, and `apply` runs it. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | with {:rule ~it} ~(get [~(count) ~(5 | apply rule)]) | eq [2 12])
    | proves :code/isAVectorOfSteps
        |~~ A quote counts its steps. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 2)
    | proves :code/readsTheStepThatWroteIt
        |~~ A quote written in a step reads the names of that step. ~~|
        ~({:bonus 10} | with [/bonus] ~(5 | apply ~(add bonus)) | eq 15)
    | proves :code/carriesTheValuesItReads
        |~~ The step writes at the head of its code the values it reads. ~~|
        ~(:vec/show (::fn | takes :code |~~ The code. ~~| /0 ::quote | impl ~(code)) | {:bonus 10} | with [/bonus] ~([1] | show ~(add bonus)) | eq ~(:bonus 10 | add bonus))
    | proves :code/withholdsAValueOfTheReader
        |~~ Held code reads no value the reader named: a rule kept in a name
            reads no value of the code around it.
        ~~|
        ~(apply ~(:bonus 10 | :rule ~(add bonus) | 5 | apply rule !| get [~(tag) ~(name)]) | eq [::call/ValueWithheldError :bonus])
    | proves :code/readsTheStepThroughAVerbOfTheLanguage
        |~~ A verb written in the language hands a quote on from the step that
            wrote it.
        ~~|
        ~(:vec/twice (::fn | takes :code |~~ The code. ~~| /0 ::quote | impl ~(map code | map code)) | {:bonus 10} | with [/bonus] ~([1 2] | twice ~(add bonus)) | eq [21 22])
    | tells :hypertext
        |~~ The hypertext. The names are a space of addresses, `::`
            beginning an absolute one, `::vec/sort`, and a verb passes
            from an address along one of its axes, as a path reaches a
            file: `doc` to its page, `binding` to its record, `source` to
            the code written under it, and `runLaws` runs the laws under
            it. An address holds no one entity but a set of
            values, and each passage reads one of them; the address, the
            verb and what it reaches are all values, so the pipeline goes
            on from what an axis reaches as from any value, and a tag you
            declare with verbs on it adds axes of its own. A keyword after
            `doc` is an anchor, the last name of a node its subject holds,
            `::qlang | doc :pipeline` the page of `::qlang/pipeline`, a
            vector of anchors read in turn, a set of anchors several pages
            at once. A page is a doc, prose that holds code: a quote
            under `::link` is a link to the page whose name its code
            answers, which `open` opens, and a quote under `::snippet` a
            query a reader runs by `answer` to see what it shows. So a
            page leads on to the pages that go deeper, and the pipeline
            moves through them as through any data: `manifest` lists what
            lies under a noun, its verbs and refusals, `links | map ~open`
            opens every page a page names in one query, and a filter over
            pages by `content | contains` searches them. Nothing is read
            that was not asked for, and a query names the shape of what it
            reads. The laws that prove a name stand under it, which
            `runLaws` holds to answer true. Rests on every concept before
            it.
            ::link~(::doc) ::link~(::link) ::link~(::snippet)
            ::link~(::qlang/hosts)
        ~~|
        #[::doc ::link ::snippet ::law]
    | proves :hypertext/opensTheLinksOfAPage
        |~~ The links of a page open in one query. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | doc | links | map ~open | count | gt 0)
    | proves :hypertext/passesIntoThreeAxes
        |~~ One address passes into its page, its record and its source. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | get [~(doc | tag) ~(binding | tag) ~(source | tag)] | eq [::doc ::binding ::quote])
    | proves :hypertext/searchesThePages
        |~~ A filter over pages finds `drop`. ~~|
        ~(::vec | manifest | filter ~(doc | content | contains "after the first") | eq #[::vec/drop])
    | tells :hosts
        |~~ Hosts. The language runs inside a host, a tool that embeds it
            and brings verbs of its own, each a name whose value is
            `::builtin{}`; the command line is one. A value is whole in
            the language, and a host prints every answer within one
            budget of characters of its print, but the REPL of the command
            line, which prints it whole: an answer that fits prints
            whole, and one past it prints as the failure
            `::qlang/AnswerBeyondBudgetError` instead, the answer the
            `::cell` the host keeps it in, whose `answer` reads it back
            whole; `print | out` writes an answer whole. A failure the host
            prints is an alert, the concentrate of its facts within two
            thousand characters, each fact whole within three hundred and
            past them as the cell the host keeps it in, beside its tag, the
            views its kind reveals that fit and its parts, `:addend/found/count`, the last facts
            dropped where the alert passes its room, a failure any fact of
            which gave way told as an `::excerpt` under the tag of its
            refusal; the command line writes below it
            the prose of its refusal's page, as `!| doc` reads it, so the
            reader repairs the call where it failed. A cell is the interface
            a host gives for the work with state: the command line keeps an
            answer past the budget and a fact past the room of a fact under
            the identity of what it keeps, and `answer` reads it in a later
            query. The command line reads JSON
            from stdin as values and other text as a string, and prints its
            answer as the language prints it, a string as its text, so
            `| json` writes JSON; its page, `::cli | doc`, tells the rest.
            Rests on values and the hypertext.
            ::link~(::qlang/AnswerBeyondBudgetError)
            ::link~(::excerpt) ::link~(::cell)
            ::link~(::builtin)
        ~~|
        #[::excerpt ::cell ::builtin]

